\section{Array Combinators State Whole-Value Dependencies}

APL and J make arrays primary values and build computations from scalar functions, reductions, scans, outer products, and rank-sensitive operators. Iverson's notation demonstrated that a short expression can name a large regular computation \cite{iverson1962apl}. J preserves this style with ASCII words. Here is an actual J definition of arithmetic mean:

\begin{verbatim}
mean =: +/ % #
mean 2 4 6 8
5
\end{verbatim}

In J's right-to-left evaluation, \texttt{\#} counts items, \texttt{+/} sums them, and the fork \texttt{+/ \% \#} divides those two results for the same argument. This is tacit programming: data arguments are omitted and combinators determine dataflow. It is not merely punctuation for a left-to-right pipeline. Rank, shape, and agreement rules decide how verbs apply to cells of an array.

\section{Retain Trains Before Expanding Element Loops}

The frontend should represent nouns, verbs, adverbs, conjunctions, explicit definitions, and tacit trains. A train node records hook or fork formation and inferred ranks; array operations retain shape transformations and scalar-extension behavior. Expanding immediately into scalar loops loses algebraic structure useful for fusion and can mishandle empty arrays or rank. The IR may later normalize a fork \(f\,g\,h\) to a graph in which \(f(y)\) and \(h(y)\) feed the dyadic \(g\).

\Cref{fig:visual-372} displays that normalization for \texttt{mean}; the fork duplicates a logical argument, not necessarily its storage.

