Compiler artifacts may include a train-preserving HIR, inferred rank and shape constraints, fused array kernels, and an executable plus spelling-aware diagnostics. Pretty-printed source is a separate artifact: canonicalizing a train can make a correct program much harder to relate to its original notation.

% Annotated walkthrough
\section{Normalize a Fork and Preserve Array Semantics}

\begin{verbatim}
train := parse_words_with_J_grammar(source)
typed := infer_valence_rank_and_shape_constraints(train)
graph := expand_hooks_and_forks_to_shared_argument_nodes(typed)
graph := fuse_array_combinators_when_rank_rules_still_match(graph)
kernel := lower_each_remaining_array_node_with_empty-case_rules()
retain source-word provenance on every graph and kernel operation
\end{verbatim}

Map fusion, scalar extension, and eliminating inverse reshape pairs can be legal. Reduction fusion needs the exact identity, order, rank, and empty-array behavior. Common-subexpression elimination is safe for pure verbs, but not for foreign conjunctions or other operations that observe external state. Copy elimination must respect aliasing if the implementation exposes mutable arrays.

Validation compares rank, shape, element values, and errors over scalars, higher-rank arrays, singleton dimensions, and empty arrays. A useful compiler test interprets the original train and normalized graph independently. For floating reductions it uses the language's specified or permitted order rather than an idealized real-number equality. This validates preservation across the tacit-to-graph handoff while still permitting different loop nests and vector instructions in the final output.

\section{Read a Fork Over Cells, Not Just Over a Flat Vector}

For the monadic fork \texttt{+/ \% \#}, the shared argument supplies both the sum and the tally. To take row means of a matrix, use the fork at rank one, so each vector row is passed to both branches. For a \(2\)-by-\(3\) matrix with rows \([2,4,6]\) and \([10,20,30]\), the means are \(4\) and \(20\). The whole-array fork is different: \texttt{+/} on that matrix inserts addition along its leading axis and \texttt{\#} tallies two leading-axis items.

\begin{center}
\begin{tabular}{llll}
\hline
Cell & Sum branch & Tally branch & Division \\
\hline
First row & 12 & 3 & 4 \\
Second row & 60 & 3 & 20 \\
Whole matrix & \(12,24,36\) & 2 & \(6,12,18\) \\
\hline
\end{tabular}
\end{center}

\begin{verbatim}
apply_fork_monad(f, g, h, argument, cell_rank):
    frame, cell_shape := split_shape(argument.shape, cell_rank)
    if frame_has_no_cells(frame):
        return empty_result_using_language_prototype(frame, cell_shape)
    results := []
    for cell in cells_in_frame_order(argument, cell_rank):
        left := apply_monad(f, cell)
        right := apply_monad(h, cell)
        append(results, apply_dyad(g, left, right))
    return assemble_with_source_shape_rules(frame, results)
\end{verbatim}

This model leaves prototype and assembly rules to the array language; those rules are not inferred from the first successful nonempty run. A frame with zero cells and a frame with nonzero cells whose shapes contain zero are different cases. In the latter, the branches must implement their own treatment of an empty argument; returning a completely empty frame may skip observable outcomes. Test non-square inputs to distinguish frames from cell axes, then add empty-axis cases. Keep rank and cell-boundary information through fusion, even when a scalar backend ultimately emits only loops and arithmetic.

\section{Test Frames Independently of Reduction Identities}

For cell rank one on a \(0\)-by-\(3\) argument, there are no row cells.
On a \(2\)-by-\(0\) argument, there are two row cells, each empty.
The same total element count does not justify merging these paths in
the compiler. For the zero-cell frame, derive the output cell shape
using the source's prototype rule. For the two empty cells, apply the
specified empty reduction and division rules to each cell. In
particular, the identity for addition does not by itself define a
meaning for division by a tally of zero.

When replacing a fork with a reduction kernel, make its lowering
report both the frame shape and where it obtained the empty-case rule.
Run a normalized-graph interpreter and a source interpreter on each
shape, comparing an error if the operator is undefined rather than
filling an arbitrary default value. That test locates a shape mistake
before a more complicated downstream computation obscures it.
